# Sign-in and permissions

Howdy Auth is set up in `src/{{.Name}}/platform/accounts.gleam`:
`identity` (sign-in by an emailed {{if .API}}code{{else}}link or code{{end}}, with registration open)
and `permissions` (roles). Both are in the app's `Services` as
`services.identity` and `services.permissions`.

Auth mounts its JSON API at `/api/auth` (`/login`, `/register`, `/me`,
`/logout`, sessions ...){{if not .API}} and its pages at `/auth/login`,
`/auth/register` and `/auth/account`{{end}}. Leave those routes to it.

## Guard a whole controller

`howdy create controller|pages|page ... --add-auth` writes this for you:

```gleam
import howdy/auth.{type Auth}
import howdy/controller

pub fn controller(db: Repo, identity: Auth) {
  controller.guarded("api/notes", auth.required(identity))
  |> controller.get("/", fn(ctx) {
    let user_id = ctx.guard.user.id           // a String
    notes.for_user(db, user_id) |> service.respond(ctx, json.array(_, note.to_json))
  })
  |> controller.build                         // required at the end
}
```

and it is registered with `howdy.controller(notes_controller.controller(db, services.identity))`.

- `ctx.guard` is a `Principal(user:, session_id:, client:)`, and
  `ctx.guard.user` a `User(id:, email:, group_id:, created_at:, updated_at:)`.
  The id is a String. Keep anything else about a person (a name, a
  profile) in your own table, keyed by that id.
- A request needs either `Authorization: Bearer <token>` or the session
  cookie; with neither it gets 401. With the cookie, a POST, PUT, PATCH or
  DELETE also needs an `Origin` header matching `APP_ORIGIN`, or it gets 403.
- Auth never redirects. {{if not .API}}A signed-out visitor to a guarded page gets a
  401; to send them to `/auth/login` instead, add middleware that turns a
  401 for a browser (an `Accept` header preferring HTML) into a 303, as
  `platform/not_found.gleam` does for 404s. {{end}}Helpers used inside a guarded
  controller should take `ctx: GuardedContext(guarded)`.

## Guard one route

```gleam
import howdy/guard

|> controller.get("/secret", fn(ctx) {
  use principal <- guard.require(ctx, auth.required(identity))
  ...
})
```

## Roles and permissions

Roles are named sets of permissions, assigned to users, checked in the
guarded controller. Pass `services.permissions` to the controller (it is
not passed by the generators), then:

```gleam
import howdy/authorization as access

use _ <- guard.require(ctx, access.require_role(permissions, "editor", access.Global))
use _ <- guard.require(ctx, access.require_permission(permissions, "notes.delete", access.Global))
```

Both answer 403 when the check fails and need a guarded context. To branch
instead: `access.has_role(permissions, ctx.guard, "editor", access.Global)`
and `access.allowed(permissions, ctx.guard, "notes.delete", access.Global)`,
each a `service.Result(Bool)`.

Define roles and grant them in code with
`access.define_role(permissions, access.Global, "editor", ["notes.write", "notes.delete"], by: user.System)`
and `access.assign(permissions, user_id, "editor", access.Global, by: user.System)`
(`howdy/auth/user` for `user.System`), or in the dev admin under Roles.
There is no built-in admin role and no wildcard.

## Sign out

`POST /api/auth/logout` with a JSON body `{}` and the session, or
`auth.logout(identity, ctx.guard)` in code.

## Test

`support.sign_in(db)` makes a user and returns a session token. Call it
once per test database, and send the token as a bearer header:

```gleam
pub fn lists_my_notes_test() {
  use db <- support.with_db()
  let app = support.app(db)
  let token = support.sign_in(db)
  let res =
    testing.get("/api/notes")
    |> testing.header("authorization", "Bearer " <> token)
    |> testing.send(app)
  assert res.status == 200
}
```

Also test that the route answers 401 without the header. The signed-in
test user is `someone@example.com`.
